# Here is the main syntax of hledger's journal format
# (omitting extra Ledger compatibility syntax).

###############################################################################

# 1. These are comment lines, for notes or temporarily disabling things.
; They begin with # or ;

comment
Or, lines can be enclosed within "comment" / "end comment".
This is a block of 
commented lines.
end comment

# Some journal entries can have semicolon comments at end of line  ; like this
# Some of them require 2 or more spaces before the semicolon.

###############################################################################

# 2. Directives customise processing or output in some way.
# You don't need any directives to get started.
# But they can add more error checking, or change how things are displayed.
# They begin with a word, letter, or symbol. 
# They are most often placed at the top, before transactions.

account assets             ; Declare valid account names and display order.
account assets:savings     ; A subaccount. This one represents a bank account.
account assets:checking    ; Another. Note, 2+ spaces after the account name.
account assets:receivable  ; Accounting type is inferred from english names,
account passifs            ; or declared with a "type" tag, type:L
account expenses           ; type:X
                           ; A follow-on comment line, indented.
account expenses:rent      ; Expense and revenue categories are also accounts.
                           ; Subaccounts inherit their parent's type.
account expenses:flat      ; test account subdirectives
    alias home             ; specify alias for account
    note This account is about home
    check commodity == USD  ; only dollars are allowed


commodity $0.00         ; Declare valid commodities and their display styles.
commodity 1.000,00 EUR
commodity CZK

decimal-mark .          ; The decimal mark used in this file (if ambiguous).

payee Whole Foods Name  ; Declare a valid payee name.

tag trip                ; Declare a valid tag name.

P 2024-03-01 AAPL $179  ; Declare a market price for AAPL in $ on this date.

include other.journal   ; Include another journal file here.

# Declare a recurring "periodic transaction", for budget/forecast reports
~ monthly  set budget goals  ; <- Note, 2+ spaces before the description.
    (expenses:rent)      $1000
    (expenses:food)       $500

# Declare an auto posting rule, to modify existing transactions in reports
= revenues:consulting
    liabilities:tax:2024:us          *0.25  ; Add a tax liability & expense
    expenses:tax:2024:us            *-0.25  ; for 25% of the revenue.

###############################################################################

# 3. Transactions are what it's all about.
# They are dated events, usually movements of money between 2 or more accounts.
# They begin with a numeric date.
# Here is their basic shape:
#
# DATE DESCRIPTION    ; The transaction's date and optional description.
#   ACCOUNT1  AMOUNT  ; A posting of an amount to/from this account, indented.
#   ACCOUNT2  AMOUNT  ; A second posting, balancing the first.
#   ...               ; More if needed. Amounts must sum to zero.
#                     ; Note, 2+ spaces between account names and amounts.

2024-01-01 opening balances         ; At the start, declare pre-existing balances this way.
    assets:savings          $10000  ; Account names can be anything. lower case is easy to type.
    assets:checking          $1000  ; assets, liabilities, equity, revenues, expenses are common.
    liabilities:credit card  $-500  ; liabilities, equity, revenues balances are usually negative.
    equity:start                    ; One amount can be left blank. $-10500 is inferred here.
                                    ; Some of these accounts we didn't declare above,
                                    ; so -s/--strict would complain.

2024-01-03 ! (12345) pay rent
    ; Additional transaction comment lines, indented.
    ; There can be a ! or * after the date meaning "pending" or "cleared".
    ; There can be a parenthesised (code) after the date/status.
                                    ; Amounts' sign shows direction of flow.
    assets:checking          $-500  ; Minus means removed from this account (credit).
    expenses:rent             $500  ; Plus means added to this account (debit).

; Keeping transactions in date order is optional (but helps error checking).

2024-01-02 Gringott's Bank | withdrawal  ; Description can be PAYEE | NOTE
    assets:bank:gold       -10 gold
    assets:pouch            10 gold

2024-01-02 shopping
    expenses:clothing        1 gold
    expenses:wands           5 gold
    assets:pouch            -6 gold

2024-01-02 receive gift
    revenues:gifts          -3 "Chocolate Frogs"  ; Complex commodity symbols
    assets:pouch             3 "Chocolate Frogs"  ; must be in double quotes.

2024-01-15 buy some shares, in two lots                 ; Cost can be noted.
    assets:investments:2024-01-15     2.0 AAAA @ $1.50  ; @  means per-unit cost
    assets:investments:2024-01-15-02  3.0 AAAA @@ $4    ; @@ means total cost
                      ; ^ Per-lot subaccounts are sometimes useful.
    assets:checking                 $-7

2024-01-15 assert some account balances on this date
    ; Balances can be asserted in any transaction, with =, for extra error checking.
    ; Assertion txns like this one can be made with hledger close --assert --show-costs
    ;
    assets:savings                    $0                   = $10000
    assets:checking                   $0                   =   $493
    assets:bank:gold                   0 gold              =    -10 gold
    assets:pouch                       0 gold              =      4 gold
    assets:pouch                       0 "Chocolate Frogs" =      3 "Chocolate Frogs"
    assets:investments:2024-01-15      0.0 AAAA            =      2.0 AAAA @  $1.50
    assets:investments:2024-01-15-02   0.0 AAAA            =      3.0 AAAA @@ $4
    liabilities:credit card           $0                   =  $-500

2024-02-01 note some event, or a transaction not yet fully entered, on this date
    ; Postings are not required.

# Consistent YYYY-MM-DD date format is recommended,
# but you can use . or / and omit leading zeros if you prefer.
2024.01.01
2024/1/1

###############################################################################

# 4. Step 2: ordinary transaction headings and descriptions.

; Secondary date
2024-01-02=2024-01-05 shopping

; Punctuation in descriptions
2024-01-02 Coffee! (large) * café #1

; Payee and note without spaces around the pipe
2024-01-02 Supermarket|weekly groceries

; Empty payee or empty note
2024-01-02 |weekly groceries
2024-01-02 Supermarket|
2024-01-02 |

; Later pipes remain part of the note
2024-01-02 Supermarket|groceries|household

; Comment immediately after description
2024-01-02 shopping;this is a comment

; Description immediately after status or code
2024-01-02 *shopping
2024-01-02 (REF123)shopping
2024-01-02 ! (REF123)shopping

###############################################################################

# 5. Step 3: amounts (regression examples).

; Commodityless amounts, including zero and explicit signs
2024-01-03 Commodityless amounts
    expenses:examples      123.45
    expenses:examples      -123.45
    expenses:examples      +123.45
    expenses:examples      0
    equity:examples

; Signs before commodities and spaces after signs
2024-01-03 Sign placement and spacing
    expenses:examples      -$1
    expenses:examples      +$1
    expenses:examples      - $1
    expenses:examples      + $1
    expenses:examples      $- 1
    expenses:examples      $+ 1
    expenses:examples      - 1 USD
    expenses:examples      + 1 USD
    equity:examples

; Scientific notation, including uppercase E and signed exponents
2024-01-03 Scientific notation
    expenses:examples      $1e3
    expenses:examples      $1E3
    expenses:examples      1.25e-3 USD
    expenses:examples      1e+3 USD
    equity:examples

; Leading and trailing decimal marks (the file declares decimal-mark .)
2024-01-03 Leading and trailing decimal points
    expenses:examples      $.5
    expenses:examples      $-.5
    expenses:examples      $1.
    equity:examples

; Each separator below is a literal Unicode character, not an ordinary space.
2024-01-03 Unicode digit grouping spaces
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+00A0 no-break space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+2002 en space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+2003 em space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+2008 punctuation space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+2009 thin space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+202F narrow no-break space
    expenses:examples      $1 000.50  ; U+205F medium mathematical space
    equity:examples

; Simple symbols can contain more than one currency character.
; Quoted digit-bearing symbols are a control for the commodity boundary.
2024-01-03 Commodity symbols
    expenses:examples      R$1
    expenses:examples      1 US$
    expenses:examples      1 "Series 2024"
    equity:examples

; Quantity must end before the cost, assertion, comment, or next posting.
2024-01-03 Amount field boundaries
    assets:examples        1e3 USD @ $2
    assets:examples        $.5 = $10
    assets:examples        1 000.50 USD  ; quantity ends before this comment
    assets:examples        123.45  ; no commodity on this line
    equity:examples

###############################################################################

# 6. Step 4: postings, costs, and balances (failing and regression examples).
# Some failures are structural: status/wrappers must not be account text,
# and continuation comments must belong to the heading or preceding posting.

; Posting status is independent of the transaction status and account name.
2024-01-04 Posting statuses
    * assets:checking     $1
    ! expenses:examples   $-1

; Balance assignments do not require an explicit posting amount.
2024-01-04 Balance assignments
    assets:assignment     = $10
    assets:assignment     == $10
    assets:assignment     =* $10
    assets:assignment     ==* $10
    equity:examples

; Single spaces belong to account names; two spaces delimit the amount.
; A semicolon inside a later account word is part of the account name.
2024-01-04 Account boundaries
    expenses:office supplies  $1
    expenses:office ;supplies  $1
    equity:examples           $-2

; The first separator below is two literal tabs; the second is a single tab
; inside the account name, followed by the usual two-space amount delimiter.
2024-01-04 Tabs in account boundaries
    assets:checking		$1
    expenses:office	supplies  $-1

; Wrappers identify virtual and balanced virtual postings, not account text.
2024-01-04 Posting types
    assets:checking        $1
    equity:examples        $-1
    (expenses:budget)      $5
    [assets:reserved]      $2
    [equity:reserved]      $-2

; Costs and assertions also accept compact field boundaries.
2024-01-04 Compact costs and assertions
    assets:shares          1 AAAA@$2
    assets:shares          1 AAAA@@$2
    assets:checking        $0=$10
    assets:checking        $0==$10
    assets:checking        $0=*$10
    assets:checking        $0==*$10
    equity:examples

; Preserve spaced operators and unit/total costs on assertion amounts.
2024-01-04 Assertion regression controls
    assets:checking        $0 = $10
    assets:checking        $0 == $10
    assets:checking        $0 =* $10
    assets:checking        $0 ==* $10
    assets:shares          0 AAAA = 2 AAAA @ $2
    assets:shares          0 AAAA = 2 AAAA @@ $4

2024-01-04 Continuation comment ownership  ; heading comment
    ; This continuation belongs to the transaction heading.
    ; This one also belongs to the heading.
    assets:checking        $1  ; first posting comment
    ; This continuation belongs to assets:checking.
    ; This one also belongs to assets:checking.
    equity:examples        $-1
    ; This continuation belongs to equity:examples.

###############################################################################

# 7. Step 5: recurring and automatic rules (failing and regression examples).
# Some failures are structural: heading fields, query terms, posting wrappers,
# and continuation comments should retain their meaning inside rules.

; Period expressions with slash/dot dates and comma-separated weekdays.
~ monthly from 2024/01/01 to 2025/01/01  Slash date range
    (expenses:budget)      $100

~ monthly from 2024.01.01 to 2025.01.01  Dot date range
    (expenses:budget)      $100

~ every mon,wed,fri  Weekday schedule
    (expenses:budget)      $10

~ every 11/05  Annual reminder
    (expenses:budget)      $10

; Regression controls for word-based intervals and relative periods.
~ every 2 weeks  Fortnightly budget
    (expenses:budget)      $100

~ quarterly in 2024  Quarterly budget
    (expenses:budget)      $100

; Two spaces separate the period from status, code, and description.
~ monthly  ! (BUDGET) Landlord|rent! (monthly)  ; heading comment
    ; This continuation belongs to the periodic heading.
    ! (expenses:rent)      $1000  ; posting comment
    ; This continuation belongs to the virtual posting.

~ monthly  * (SAVE) Savings transfer
    [assets:reserved]      $100
    [equity:reserved]      $-100

; A description is optional, including when a heading has a comment.
~ monthly  ; heading comment without a description
    (expenses:budget)      $100

; Whitespace after the rule marker is optional or can be multiple spaces.
~monthly  Compact marker
    (expenses:budget)      $100

~   monthly  Spaced marker
    (expenses:budget)      $100

; Multiple query terms, including quotes containing spaces.
= expenses:food not:desc:refund
    (expenses:budget)      *1

= acct:expenses desc:"weekly groceries" not:tag:skip
    (expenses:budget)      *1

; Rule comments and posting continuation comments have separate owners.
= expenses:food  ; automatic rule comment
    ; This continuation belongs to the automatic rule heading.
    (expenses:budget)      *1  ; posting comment
    ; This continuation belongs to the virtual posting.

; Automatic postings share ordinary posting fields and optional amounts.
= expenses:examples
    ! assets:checking     $1
    * (expenses:budget)   *0.25
    [assets:reserved]     *1
    [equity:reserved]     *-1
    assets:shares         1 AAAA @ $2
    assets:checking       $0 = $10
    assets:assignment     = $10
    equity:examples

; Multiplier controls: bare/signed quantities and commodity-bearing amounts.
= expenses:examples
    (expenses:budget)      *1
    (expenses:budget)      *-0.25
    (expenses:budget)      *$0.25
    (expenses:budget)      $1

; Automatic rule markers also allow zero or multiple following spaces.
=expenses:food
    (expenses:budget)      *1

=   expenses:food
    (expenses:budget)      *1

###############################################################################

# 8. Step 6: directives (failing and regression examples).
# These exercise syntax only; include targets need not exist for tree-sitter.
# Indented content must belong to its directive, and fields must stay on one line.

; Default year spellings, including the compact single-letter form.
Y 2024
Y2024
year 2024
apply year 2024
01-06 Yearless date after a default year

; A default commodity format includes a decimal mark.
D $1,000.00

; Basic and regular-expression aliases, followed by their terminator.
alias cash = assets:cash
alias /^expenses:food/ = expenses:groceries
end aliases

; Account scopes can nest; each terminator closes the preceding scope.
apply account assets
apply account savings
2024-01-06 Scoped account names
    bank                  $1
    cash                  $-1
end apply account
end apply account

; Optional directive prefixes directly precede the keyword.
!account assets:prefixed
@account expenses:prefixed
!D $1.00
@year 2024
!alias wallet = assets:wallet
@end aliases

; Commodity format subdirectives and other accepted indented content.
commodity USD  ; declaration comment
    ; continuation comment
    format 1,000.00 USD  ; display format
    note US dollars
    custom metadata accepted by the reader
commodity EUR
    format 1.000,00 EUR
    format 1,000.00 EUR

; Account subdirectives are not a closed, validated list in hledger.
account expenses:directive examples  ; type:X
    alias household spending
    note A note with spaces
    check commodity == USD
    assert commodity == USD
    type expense
    custom metadata accepted by the reader

; Payee declarations accept quoted names, including the empty name.
payee "Example Shop"
    alias Example.*Shop
    custom payee metadata
payee ""
payee Example|Shop

; Tags also accept continuation comments and arbitrary indented content.
tag project  ; declaration comment
    ; continuation comment
    custom tag metadata

; Include paths may contain spaces and globs, with optional reader prefixes.
include journals/household budget.journal
include journals/monthly reports/*.journal  ; path ends before the semicolon
include journal:journals/household budget.journal
include csv:imports/bank statement.csv
include timeclock:work logs/2024.timeclock
include timedot:work logs/2024.timedot

; Prices accept optional times, seconds, and timezone offsets.
P 2024-01-06 AAPL $179
P 2024-01-06 09:30 AAPL $179
P 2024-01-06 09:30:45 AAPL $179
P 2024-01-06 09:30-0800 AAPL $179
P 2024-01-06 09:30:45+0130 AAPL $179
P2024-01-06 AAPL $179

; Adjacent directives and a transaction must remain separate journal items.
decimal-mark .
commodity GBP
payee Boundary Shop
tag boundary
include boundary.journal
P 2024-01-06 GBP $1.25
2024-01-06 Directive boundary sentinel
    assets:checking       $1
    equity:examples       $-1
